```java
@Component
public class JwtUtil {

    @Value("${jwt.secret}")
    private String secret;  // At least 256 bits / 32 chars for HS256

    @Value("${jwt.expiration-ms}")
    private long expirationMs;  // e.g., 900000 = 15 minutes

    private SecretKey getSigningKey() {
        return Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(Decoders.BASE64.decode(secret));
    }

    // Generate JWT
    public String generateToken(UserDetails userDetails) {
        Map<String, Object> claims = new HashMap<>();
        claims.put("roles", userDetails.getAuthorities().stream()
            .map(GrantedAuthority::getAuthority)
            .collect(Collectors.toList()));

        return Jwts.builder()
            .claims(claims)
            .subject(userDetails.getUsername())
            .issuedAt(new Date())
            .expiration(new Date(System.currentTimeMillis() + expirationMs))
            .signWith(getSigningKey())
            .compact();
    }

    // Extract username from token
    public String extractUsername(String token) {
        return extractClaim(token, Claims::getSubject);
    }

    // Extract any claim
    public <T> T extractClaim(String token, Function<Claims, T> claimsResolver) {
        final Claims claims = extractAllClaims(token);
        return claimsResolver.apply(claims);
    }

    private Claims extractAllClaims(String token) {
        return Jwts.parser()
            .verifyWith(getSigningKey())
            .build()
            .parseSignedClaims(token)
            .getPayload();
        // Throws: JwtException if signature invalid, ExpiredJwtException if expired
    }

    // Validate token
    public boolean isTokenValid(String token, UserDetails userDetails) {
        try {
            final String username = extractUsername(token);
            return username.equals(userDetails.getUsername()) && !isTokenExpired(token);
        } catch (JwtException e) {
            return false;  // Invalid signature, malformed, etc.
        }
    }

    private boolean isTokenExpired(String token) {
        return extractClaim(token, Claims::getExpiration).before(new Date());
    }
}
```

## HMAC vs RSA Signing

```java
// HMAC (HS256) — symmetric: same key for sign and verify
// Good for: monolith or single service
// Risk: any service that verifies can also forge tokens

// RSA (RS256) — asymmetric
// Private key: signs tokens (auth service only)
// Public key: verifies tokens (all other services can verify, can't forge)

// RSA in JwtUtil:
private PrivateKey privateKey;  // Loaded from keystore
private PublicKey publicKey;

String token = Jwts.builder()
    .subject(username)
    .signWith(privateKey, Jwts.SIG.RS256)
    .compact();

Claims claims = Jwts.parser()
    .verifyWith(publicKey)  // Any service can have the public key
    .build()
    .parseSignedClaims(token)
    .getPayload();
```

## Production Reality

Store the JWT secret in a secrets manager (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault) — not in application.properties files that get committed to Git. Rotate keys periodically. For microservices, use RSA: the auth service holds the private key, other services get the public key via a JWKS endpoint (`/.well-known/jwks.json`) and verify tokens without contacting the auth service.

## RefreshToken Entity

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "refresh_tokens")
public class RefreshToken {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    @OneToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id", referencedColumnName = "id")
    private User user;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String token;  // Random UUID or hash(random)

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private LocalDateTime expiresAt;

    private boolean revoked = false;

    public boolean isExpired() {
        return expiresAt.isBefore(LocalDateTime.now());
    }

    public boolean isValid() {
        return !revoked && !isExpired();
    }
}
```

## RefreshToken Service

```java
@Service
@Transactional
public class RefreshTokenService {

    @Value("${jwt.refresh-expiration-days:7}")
    private int refreshExpirationDays;

    public RefreshToken createRefreshToken(Long userId) {
        // Invalidate any existing tokens for this user
        refreshTokenRepo.deleteByUserId(userId);

        RefreshToken token = new RefreshToken();
        token.setUser(userRepo.findById(userId).orElseThrow());
        token.setToken(UUID.randomUUID().toString());  // Opaque token
        token.setExpiresAt(LocalDateTime.now().plusDays(refreshExpirationDays));
        return refreshTokenRepo.save(token);
    }

    public RefreshToken validateAndRotate(String tokenValue) {
        RefreshToken token = refreshTokenRepo.findByToken(tokenValue)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new RefreshTokenException("Invalid refresh token"));

        if (!token.isValid()) {
            if (token.isRevoked()) {
                // Token already used — possible theft!
                // Revoke ALL tokens for this user
                refreshTokenRepo.revokeAllByUserId(token.getUser().getId());
                throw new RefreshTokenException("Refresh token reuse detected. Please re-login.");
            }
            if (token.isExpired()) {
                refreshTokenRepo.delete(token);
                throw new RefreshTokenException("Refresh token expired. Please re-login.");
            }
        }

        // Mark old token as revoked (for theft detection)
        token.setRevoked(true);
        refreshTokenRepo.save(token);

        // Issue new refresh token (rotation)
        return createRefreshToken(token.getUser().getId());
    }
}
```

## Auth Controller — Login + Refresh

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/auth")
public class AuthController {

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public ResponseEntity<AuthResponse> login(
            @RequestBody LoginRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response) {
        Authentication auth = authenticationManager.authenticate(
            new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(request.getEmail(), request.getPassword())
        );
        UserDetails userDetails = (UserDetails) auth.getPrincipal();

        String accessToken = jwtUtil.generateToken(userDetails);  // 15 min
        RefreshToken refreshToken = refreshTokenService.createRefreshToken(
            ((CustomUserDetails) userDetails).getUserId()
        );

        // Store refresh token in HttpOnly cookie (XSS-safe)
        Cookie cookie = new Cookie("refreshToken", refreshToken.getToken());
        cookie.setHttpOnly(true);
        cookie.setSecure(true);      // HTTPS only
        cookie.setPath("/api/auth"); // Only sent to refresh endpoint
        cookie.setMaxAge(7 * 24 * 60 * 60);  // 7 days
        response.addCookie(cookie);

        return ResponseEntity.ok(new AuthResponse(accessToken));  // Return access token in body
    }

    @PostMapping("/refresh")
    public ResponseEntity<AuthResponse> refreshToken(
            @CookieValue("refreshToken") String refreshTokenValue,
            HttpServletResponse response) {
        RefreshToken newRefreshToken = refreshTokenService.validateAndRotate(refreshTokenValue);

        UserDetails userDetails = userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(
            newRefreshToken.getUser().getEmail()
        );
        String newAccessToken = jwtUtil.generateToken(userDetails);

        // Update the refresh token cookie with new token
        Cookie cookie = new Cookie("refreshToken", newRefreshToken.getToken());
        cookie.setHttpOnly(true);
        cookie.setSecure(true);
        cookie.setPath("/api/auth");
        cookie.setMaxAge(7 * 24 * 60 * 60);
        response.addCookie(cookie);

        return ResponseEntity.ok(new AuthResponse(newAccessToken));
    }

    @PostMapping("/logout")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> logout(
            @CookieValue(value = "refreshToken", required = false) String refreshTokenValue,
            HttpServletResponse response) {
        if (refreshTokenValue != null) {
            refreshTokenRepo.findByToken(refreshTokenValue)
                .ifPresent(token -> refreshTokenRepo.deleteByUserId(token.getUser().getId()));
        }
        // Clear the cookie
        Cookie cookie = new Cookie("refreshToken", "");
        cookie.setMaxAge(0);
        cookie.setPath("/api/auth");
        response.addCookie(cookie);
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Store the refresh token in an `HttpOnly` cookie scoped to the refresh endpoint path — this protects it from XSS while limiting its CSRF exposure. The access token goes in localStorage or memory on the client — it's short-lived so exposure is limited. Add CSRF protection specifically on the `/refresh` endpoint if using cookie-based refresh tokens. Implement scheduled cleanup of expired refresh tokens in the database.

## Comprehensive JWT Validation

```java
@Component
public class JwtValidator {

    @Value("${jwt.secret}")
    private String secret;

    @Value("${jwt.issuer:myapp}")
    private String expectedIssuer;

    private SecretKey signingKey() {
        return Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(Decoders.BASE64.decode(secret));
    }

    public JwtValidationResult validate(String token) {
        try {
            Claims claims = Jwts.parser()
                .verifyWith(signingKey())
                .requireIssuer(expectedIssuer)   // Validate issuer
                .clockSkewSeconds(30)             // Allow 30s clock drift
                .build()
                .parseSignedClaims(token)
                .getPayload();

            // Claims validated automatically: exp, nbf, iss
            // Additional business validation:
            if (claims.getSubject() == null || claims.getSubject().isBlank()) {
                return JwtValidationResult.invalid("Missing subject claim");
            }

            return JwtValidationResult.valid(claims);

        } catch (ExpiredJwtException e) {
            // Token valid but expired — suggest refresh
            return JwtValidationResult.expired(e.getClaims());

        } catch (SignatureException e) {
            // Signature mismatch — possible tampering
            log.warn("JWT signature validation failed — possible token tampering");
            return JwtValidationResult.invalid("Invalid signature");

        } catch (MalformedJwtException e) {
            return JwtValidationResult.invalid("Malformed token");

        } catch (JwtException e) {
            return JwtValidationResult.invalid("Token validation failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

public sealed interface JwtValidationResult permits Valid, Expired, Invalid {
    static JwtValidationResult valid(Claims claims) { return new Valid(claims); }
    static JwtValidationResult expired(Claims claims) { return new Expired(claims); }
    static JwtValidationResult invalid(String reason) { return new Invalid(reason); }

    record Valid(Claims claims) implements JwtValidationResult {}
    record Expired(Claims claims) implements JwtValidationResult {}
    record Invalid(String reason) implements JwtValidationResult {}
}
```

## Filter with Proper Error Handling

```java
@Override
protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request,
                                 HttpServletResponse response,
                                 FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {
    String token = extractToken(request);
    if (token == null) {
        chain.doFilter(request, response);
        return;
    }

    JwtValidationResult result = jwtValidator.validate(token);

    switch (result) {
        case Valid valid -> {
            String username = valid.claims().getSubject();
            UserDetails user = userDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username);
            var auth = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(
                user, null, user.getAuthorities()
            );
            SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(auth);
        }
        case Expired expired -> {
            // Let ExceptionTranslationFilter handle it
            // Or set WWW-Authenticate header:
            response.setHeader("WWW-Authenticate",
                "Bearer error=\"invalid_token\", error_description=\"Token expired\"");
            // Don't set authentication — request will be rejected as 401
        }
        case Invalid invalid -> {
            // Malformed or tampered — don't authenticate
            log.debug("Invalid JWT: {}", invalid.reason());
        }
    }
    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}

// Client can check WWW-Authenticate header to decide: refresh or re-login
```

## Token Expiry Strategy

```java
// Recommended expiry times:
// Access token: 15 minutes (frequent refresh is fine — it's fast)
// Refresh token: 7-30 days (balance convenience vs security)

// For high-security (banking, healthcare): access = 5 min, refresh = 8 hours
// For low-security (content site): access = 1 hour, refresh = 30 days

// Add 'jti' (JWT ID) claim for potential revocation:
String jti = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
String token = Jwts.builder()
    .id(jti)       // Unique token ID
    .subject(username)
    .expiration(expiry)
    .signWith(signingKey())
    .compact();

// To revoke: add jti to Redis blacklist until expiry time
// Check blacklist in filter before authenticating
```

## Production Reality

Keep access token lifetime short — 15 minutes is the industry standard. Implement transparent token refresh in the frontend: when a 401 response is received, automatically try the refresh endpoint and retry the original request. This gives users an experience of never needing to re-login during active sessions, while keeping the actual access token short-lived.

## Opaque Token Implementation

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "access_tokens")
public class AccessToken {
    @Id
    private String token;  // Random UUID or cryptographic random string

    @ManyToOne
    private User user;

    private LocalDateTime expiresAt;
    private boolean revoked = false;

    // Scopes for OAuth-like systems:
    @ElementCollection
    private Set<String> scopes = new HashSet<>();
}

@Service
public class TokenValidationService {
    @Autowired
    private AccessTokenRepository tokenRepo;
    @Autowired
    private RedisTemplate<String, Object> redis;  // Cache layer

    public Optional<User> validateToken(String token) {
        // Check cache first (avoid DB hit for every request)
        Object cached = redis.opsForValue().get("token:" + token);
        if (cached != null) {
            if ("REVOKED".equals(cached)) return Optional.empty();
            return Optional.of((User) cached);
        }

        // Cache miss: query DB
        return tokenRepo.findByTokenAndRevokedFalse(token)
            .filter(t -> !t.getExpiresAt().isBefore(LocalDateTime.now()))
            .map(t -> {
                // Cache for remaining TTL
                Duration ttl = Duration.between(LocalDateTime.now(), t.getExpiresAt());
                redis.opsForValue().set("token:" + token, t.getUser(), ttl);
                return t.getUser();
            });
    }

    public void revokeToken(String token) {
        tokenRepo.findByToken(token).ifPresent(t -> {
            t.setRevoked(true);
            tokenRepo.save(t);
        });
        // Immediately update cache
        redis.opsForValue().set("token:" + token, "REVOKED", Duration.ofHours(1));
    }

    public void revokeAllForUser(Long userId) {
        List<AccessToken> tokens = tokenRepo.findByUserId(userId);
        tokens.forEach(t -> {
            t.setRevoked(true);
            redis.opsForValue().set("token:" + t.getToken(), "REVOKED", Duration.ofHours(1));
        });
        tokenRepo.saveAll(tokens);
        // All sessions invalidated instantly — even active ones
    }
}
```

## Comparison Table

```
                    | JWT              | Opaque Token
--------------------+------------------+------------------
Verification        | Local (no DB)    | DB or cache lookup
Revocation          | Impossible*      | Instant
Payload visibility  | Readable (B64)   | Server-side only
Stateless           | Yes              | No
Scalability         | High             | Depends on cache
Microservices       | Excellent        | Needs central store
Implementation      | Simple           | More complex
Sensitive data      | Avoid in payload | Safe (server-side)

* JWT revocation requires blacklist (adds state)
```

## Hybrid Approach: JWT + Opaque Refresh

```
Best of both worlds:
  Access token: JWT (short-lived, stateless, fast verification)
  Refresh token: Opaque (long-lived, DB-stored, instantly revocable)

  JWT expires frequently → minimal revocation window
  Opaque refresh: can be revoked at any time → effective logout
  On compromise: revoke refresh token → user must re-login after JWT expires

Max exposure window = JWT lifetime (15 min with short-lived access tokens)
```

## Production Reality

This hybrid approach (JWT access + opaque refresh) is the industry standard for most production APIs. It combines stateless verification speed with the security of revocable refresh tokens. For regulated industries requiring instant access revocation, add a Redis-based JWT blacklist keyed by `jti` claim — add the `jti` on logout/revocation and check it in the JWT validation filter.
